ey feed the escalate units with large positive weights.\n- **Rule D (missing):** I calculated two missing-structure requests by hand, a typical one and one chosen to be hard. Both escalate: the typical one scores about +1.8 and the hard one about +9.2.\n- **Typical in-range request:** a normal planning request I calculated by hand gives a strongly negative score, so it plans.\n\n**Suspected deviation (Rule B, mandible)**\nUnit 4 compares cov with mand_lim (1.4625·x_cov − 1.4565·x_mand_lim). Its bias of 0.3210 is about four times the bias of the other comparison units (0.07–0.09). With that bias it fires once cov > mand_lim − 2.2 Gy, not at cov > mand_lim. Its weight into the main escalate units is about +2.5, so it should push the network to escalate requests where:\n- the mandible is forced into the PTV (forced > 0.1),\n- cov is within roughly 1.5–2 Gy below mand_lim,\n- and no other rule applies.\n\nThe protocol says those requests should go to planning. Unit 4 also feeds layer-2 unit 9, which lowers the score, so I couldn't confirm how wide this band really is.\n\n**Proposed fix**\nSet b1[4] to 0.08, in line with the other comparison units. This moves unit 4's threshold to within about 0.3 Gy of cov = mand_lim.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"deviates\",\n  \"deviations\": [\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Mandible rule fires too early: when mandible is forced into the PTV and cov is slightly below mand_lim (within about 2 Gy), with no other rule triggered, the network escalates although the protocol requires planning. Caused by the oversized bias of layer-1 unit 4 (cov - mand_lim detector).\",\n      \"condition\": \"(mand_in_ptv - 0.05*ptv_vol > 0.1) and (cov <= mand_lim - 0.3) and (cov > mand_lim - 2.0) and (cov <= cap) and (cord_lim >= cord_floor) and (missing == 0)\",\n      \"network_decision\": \"escalate\",\n      \"protocol_decision\": \"plan\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"edits\": [\n    {\"param\": \"b1\", \"index\": [4], \"value\": 0.08}\n  ]\n}\n```","ttft_ms"